= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose <Minkowski metric> $(+,-,-,-)$, so $A_\mu=(\phi/c,-\mathbf A)$ and $d\tau=dt\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}$. In laboratory time the <relativistic charged-particle action> has <Lagrangian>
$$
L=-mc^2\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}-e\phi(\mathbf x,t)+e\mathbf A(\mathbf x,t)\cdot\mathbf v.
$$
Here $e$ denotes the signed particle charge in the action. The <canonical momentum> and the position derivative are
$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial v_i}=m\gamma v_i+eA_i,\qquad
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_i}=-e\partial_i\phi+e v_j\partial_iA_j,
\qquad \gamma=(1-v^2/c^2)^{-1/2}.
$$
The <Euler-Lagrange equation> therefore gives
$$
\frac d{dt}(m\gamma v_i)=e\left[-\partial_i\phi-\partial_t A_i+v_j(\partial_iA_j-\partial_jA_i)\right].
$$
The last term is $(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)_i$, and the first two terms form the <electric field>. Thus the <relativistic Lorentz force> equation is
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}(m\gamma\mathbf v)=e(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B).}
$$
Its power relation and acceleration form are
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}(\gamma mc^2)=e\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v,\qquad
\dot{\mathbf v}=\frac e{m\gamma}\left[\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B-\frac{\mathbf v(\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v)}{c^2}\right].}
$$
The power relation follows by taking the scalar product with $\mathbf v$ and using the <relativistic energy-momentum relation>. It also shows why the <magnetic field> alone cannot change the particle's energy.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a uniform constant <electric field> and zero <magnetic field>, the <mechanical momentum> changes linearly even though the velocity does not:
$$
\mathbf p(t)=\mathbf p_0+e\mathbf E t,\qquad \mathbf p_0=m\gamma_0\mathbf v_0,\qquad
\gamma_0=(1-v_0^2/c^2)^{-1/2}.
$$
Invert $\mathbf p=m\gamma\mathbf v$ using $\gamma=\sqrt{1+p^2/(m^2c^2)}$. The result for arbitrary initial direction is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v(t)=\frac{\gamma_0\mathbf v_0+(e\mathbf E/m)t}
{\sqrt{1+\left|\gamma_0\mathbf v_0+(e\mathbf E/m)t\right|^2/c^2}}.}
$$
Equivalently, $\mathbf v=c\mathbf p/\sqrt{m^2c^2+p^2}$. This gives $\mathbf v(0)=\mathbf v_0$ and guarantees $|\mathbf v|<c$ at every finite momentum. The transverse momentum stays constant, but its velocity component generally decreases because the <Lorentz factor> increases. Thus an initial transverse velocity must not be treated as constant during the electric acceleration; this is the <relativistic motion in a constant electric field with transverse momentum> effect.

\Image[2018-q1-electric-velocity.png]
{title=Constant electric force changes momentum linearly while speed stays below light speed}
{description=Original example with initial velocity perpendicular to the electric force and magnitude $0.6c$. The longitudinal velocity tends to $c$, while the transverse velocity tends to zero. Time is scaled by $mc/(|e|E)$, and the positive longitudinal direction is chosen along the force.}

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

If $e\mathbf E\ne0$, then $\mathbf p(t)=e\mathbf E t+O(1)$, and the <relativistic energy-momentum relation> gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbf v(t)=c\frac{e\mathbf E}{|e\mathbf E|}.}
$$
The particle approaches the <speed of light> in the direction of the electric force, independently of its initial velocity. For positive $e$ the limiting direction is along $\mathbf E$; for negative $e$ it is opposite. A massive particle never reaches that limiting speed in finite time. If the force is zero, the velocity remains $\mathbf v_0$ instead.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

With no <electric field>, the power relation from part (a) gives
$$
\frac d{dt}(\gamma mc^2)=e\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)=0.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\gamma(t)=\gamma_0,\qquad |\mathbf v(t)|=|\mathbf v_0|.}
$$
The magnetic part of the <relativistic Lorentz force> changes the direction of <mechanical momentum>, but performs no work. This conclusion does not require the <magnetic field> to be spatially uniform: it follows from the local orthogonality of $\mathbf v$ and $\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Let $\mathbf b=\mathbf B/B$ for a nonzero constant <magnetic field>. Since $\gamma$ is constant, the <relativistic Lorentz force> reduces to
$$
\dot{\mathbf v}=\frac e{m\gamma_0}\mathbf v\times\mathbf B.
$$
The projection along $\mathbf b$ is constant. In the perpendicular plane define the signed <cyclotron frequency> $\Omega=eB/(m\gamma_0)$; then
$$
\dot{\mathbf v}_\perp=\Omega\mathbf v_\perp\times\mathbf b,\qquad
\ddot{\mathbf v}_\perp=-\Omega^2\mathbf v_\perp.
$$
The explicit rotating velocity is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v_\perp(t)=\mathbf v_{\perp0}\cos(\Omega t)
+(\mathbf v_{\perp0}\times\mathbf b)\sin(\Omega t),\qquad
T=\frac{2\pi\gamma_0m}{|e|B}.}
$$
This is the <relativistic cyclotron orbit> frequency in laboratory time, reduced from the nonrelativistic frequency by $1/\gamma_0$. With nonzero parallel velocity, the trajectory is a helix. If $\mathbf v_{\perp0}=0$, that perpendicular vector stays zero and has no nontrivial fundamental period; the displayed $T$ is the period of a nonzero transverse orbit.

