= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the downward $y$ direction of the PDF diagram. The bob's Cartesian coordinates are
$$
x=s+\ell\sin\theta,\qquad y=\ell\cos\theta.
$$
Thus
$$
\dot x=\dot s+\ell\dot\theta\cos\theta,\qquad
\dot y=-\ell\dot\theta\sin\theta.
$$
The hinge and rod are massless, so only the bob contributes <kinetic energy>:
$$
T=\frac m2(\dot x^2+\dot y^2)
=\frac m2\left(\dot s^2+2\ell\dot s\dot\theta\cos\theta+\ell^2\dot\theta^2\right).
$$
Because gravity points along positive $y$, its <gravitational potential energy> is $-mgy=-mg\ell\cos\theta$. The zero-rest-length <spring> has <elastic potential energy> $ks^2/2$. Put $s=\ell\eta$ and divide $T-V$ by the positive constant $m\ell^2$. The equivalent <Lagrangian> is
$$
\boxed{L=\frac12\dot\eta^2+\dot\eta\dot\theta\cos\theta+\frac12\dot\theta^2
+\frac g\ell\cos\theta-\frac k{2m}\eta^2.}
$$
Multiplying the <action> by a nonzero constant leaves its stationary paths unchanged. This derivation is a direct use of <kinetic energy from Cartesian position vectors>; no independent kinetic term should be assigned to the massless support.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the horizontal <generalized coordinate> $\eta$,
$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot\eta}=\dot\eta+\dot\theta\cos\theta,\qquad
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\eta}=-\frac km\eta.
$$
The corresponding <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\ddot\eta+\ddot\theta\cos\theta-\dot\theta^2\sin\theta+\frac km\eta=0.
$$
For $\theta$, the terms proportional to $\dot\eta\dot\theta\sin\theta$ cancel between $d(\partial L/\partial\dot\theta)/dt$ and $\partial L/\partial\theta$. Hence the other exact equation is
$$
\ddot\theta+\ddot\eta\cos\theta+\frac g\ell\sin\theta=0.
$$
Keeping only terms linear in small coordinates and their derivatives gives
$$
\boxed{\ddot\theta+\ddot\eta+\omega_0^2\theta=0,\qquad
\ddot\theta+\ddot\eta+\omega_1^2\eta=0,\qquad
\omega_0=\sqrt{\frac g\ell},\quad\omega_1=\sqrt{\frac km}.}
$$
The equilibrium is $\theta=\eta=0$. These characteristic frequencies label gravitational and spring restoring scales; they are not two independent <normal mode> frequencies of this massless-support system.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Subtracting the two linearized equations eliminates their acceleration terms:
$$
\omega_0^2\theta=\omega_1^2\eta.
$$
For $g,k>0$, the assumed nontrivial <simple harmonic motion> therefore has
$$
\boxed{\eta(t)=\frac{\omega_0^2}{\omega_1^2}\theta_0\sin(\omega t)
=\frac{mg}{k\ell}\theta_0\sin(\omega t).}
$$
The support displacement and pendulum angle oscillate in phase. Inserting this relation into either <Euler-Lagrange equation> yields
$$
\omega^2\left(1+\frac{\omega_0^2}{\omega_1^2}\right)=\omega_0^2,
$$
so the one positive finite <angular frequency> is
$$
\boxed{\omega=\frac{\omega_0\omega_1}{\sqrt{\omega_0^2+\omega_1^2}}
=\sqrt{\frac{gk}{mg+k\ell}},\qquad \frac1{\omega^2}=\frac\ell g+\frac mk.}
$$
The negative frequency describes the same real oscillation after a phase change. For a general phase, replace the sine in both coordinates by $A\cos(\omega t)+B\sin(\omega t)$.

There is no missing second finite branch. The quadratic <Lagrangian> is
$$
L_2=\frac12(\dot\eta+\dot\theta)^2-\frac12\omega_1^2\eta^2-\frac12\omega_0^2\theta^2,
$$
whose kinetic matrix is $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ and has rank one. Its characteristic <determinant> is $\omega_0^2\omega_1^2-(\omega_0^2+\omega_1^2)\omega^2$, not a quadratic polynomial in $\omega^2$. This <singular kinetic matrix in small oscillations> results from taking the support mass to be exactly zero. Adding a small support mass restores a second mode that runs to infinite frequency as that mass vanishes.

For a stiff spring,
$$
\boxed{k\to\infty:\qquad\eta\to0,\qquad\omega\to\sqrt{g/\ell}.}
$$
The hinge becomes fixed, recovering the <simple pendulum> frequency, as expected for the <pendulum with a massless spring-mounted support>. At finite $k$, horizontal support motion makes the frequency smaller.

\Image[2018-q2-massless-support-frequency.png]
{title=A massless spring-mounted support lowers the pendulum's one finite frequency}
{description=Original curve of $\omega/\omega_0=r/\sqrt{1+r^2}$ against $r=\omega_1/\omega_0$. The small-$r$ spring-controlled limit and the large-$r$ fixed-support limit are marked.}

