= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

If $L'=L+dF(q,t)/dt$, the <action> changes by an endpoint term:
$$
S'[q]-S[q]=F(q(t_f),t_f)-F(q(t_i),t_i).
$$
In the <principle of stationary action> with fixed endpoints, $\delta q(t_i)=\delta q(t_f)=0$ and the endpoint times are fixed. Therefore $\delta(S'-S)=0$, and the two actions give the same bulk <Euler-Lagrange equations>.

For a <Lagrangian density>, adding $\partial_\mu K^\mu$ changes the <action> by $\int_{\partial\Omega}K^\mu n_\mu\,d\Sigma$, by the <divergence theorem>. Its variation vanishes for compactly supported field variations, or when the appropriate boundary data are held fixed. These <total divergences in a variational principle> can therefore be discarded when deriving bulk equations under those conditions.

The qualification about boundaries matters: if $K^\mu$ depends on field derivatives, their variations on the boundary must also be controlled. A <total derivative> can change the canonical momenta or the chosen representative of a <Noether current>, even while leaving the bulk dynamics unchanged.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $P^\mu=\partial\mathcal L/\partial(\partial_\mu h)$ and $Q^{\mu\nu}=\partial\mathcal L/\partial(\partial_\mu\partial_\nu h)$. Define the second coefficient with ordered repeated indices and $Q^{\mu\nu}=Q^{\nu\mu}$, so that
$$
\delta\mathcal L=\mathcal L_h\delta h+P^\mu\partial_\mu\delta h+Q^{\mu\nu}\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\delta h.
$$
This convention avoids double-counting a mixed derivative such as $h_{,12}=h_{,21}$. The first derivative term needs one <integration by parts>. The second derivative term needs two:
$$
\int_\Omega Q^{\mu\nu}\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\delta h
=\text{boundary terms}+\int_\Omega(\partial_\mu\partial_\nu Q^{\mu\nu})\delta h.
$$
Combining the terms gives
$$
\delta S=\int_\Omega\left[\mathcal L_h-\partial_\mu P^\mu+\partial_\mu\partial_\nu Q^{\mu\nu}\right]\delta h
+\int_{\partial\Omega}n_\mu\left[(P^\mu-\partial_\nu Q^{\mu\nu})\delta h+Q^{\mu\nu}\partial_\nu\delta h\right]d\Sigma.
$$
For compactly supported variations, or fixed $\delta h$ and its first derivatives at the boundary, the last integral vanishes. The <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> then yields the <Euler-Lagrange field equation with second derivatives>:
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial h}
-\partial_\mu\left[\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu h)}\right]
+\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\left[\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\partial_\nu h)}\right]=0.}
$$
The plus sign of the final term comes from the two integrations by parts. In general this gives fourth-order equations, although the order in a particular coordinate depends on which second derivatives occur in the density.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $R=h_t-\nu\nabla^2h$, where the <diffusion coefficient> $\nu$ is constant. Then
$$
\delta S=2\int R(\partial_t\delta h-\nu\nabla^2\delta h)\,d^2x\,dt.
$$
One temporal and two spatial <integrations by parts> give
$$
\delta S=-2\int(\partial_tR+\nu\nabla^2R)\delta h\,d^2x\,dt,
$$
with compactly supported variations. Hence
$$
\boxed{(\partial_t+\nu\nabla^2)(\partial_t-\nu\nabla^2)h=0,
\qquad h_{tt}-\nu^2\nabla^4h=0.}
$$
Here $\nabla^4=(\nabla^2)^2$ is the <biharmonic operator>. The cross terms cancel because $\nu$ is constant and spatial and temporal derivatives commute. Equivalently, $P^0=2R$ and $Q^{ij}=-2\nu R\delta^{ij}$ in part (b), producing $-2\partial_tR-2\nu\nabla^2R=0$.

This <squared diffusion-residual action> does not have only the first-order <diffusion equation> as its stationary equation. The zero-residual paths $h_t=\nu\nabla^2h$ have $S=0$ and are certainly solutions, but the variational equation is the higher-order one above. For a spatial <Fourier mode> with nonzero <wavevector> $\mathbf k$, it gives
$$
\ddot h_{\mathbf k}-\nu^2|\mathbf k|^4h_{\mathbf k}=0,\qquad
h_{\mathbf k}(t)=A_{\mathbf k}e^{-\nu|\mathbf k|^2t}+B_{\mathbf k}e^{+\nu|\mathbf k|^2t}.
$$
Boundary data or an additional physical selection principle are needed to retain only the decaying diffusion branch. The spatially uniform mode instead has $h_0(t)=A_0+B_0t$ in the stationary equation.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

With constant $\nu$, the bulk <action> is invariant under time translations, translations in the two spatial coordinates, and rotations or reflections within the substrate plane. The <Laplacian> has no preferred direction, giving the planar $O(2)$ symmetry. It also has <scalar-field shift symmetry> $h\mapsto h+a$, expressing the arbitrary origin of height, and the discrete symmetry $h\mapsto-h$ because its residual is squared.

There is an additional exact tilt symmetry $h\mapsto h+\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf x$: the added slope is time independent and has zero <Laplacian>. More generally, adding any function $f$ satisfying $f_t=\nu\nabla^2f$ leaves the residual unchanged. Such bulk transformations need not preserve prescribed boundary conditions or a restricted physical interpretation of the height.

In two spatial dimensions the <action> also has the anisotropic <scale invariance>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x\mapsto b\mathbf x,\qquad t\mapsto b^2t,\qquad h\mapsto h\quad(b>0).}
$$
The spacetime measure scales as $b^4$ and the squared residual as $b^{-4}$. The time and length scales are therefore related diffusively, rather than by a Lorentz transformation.

Ordinary <time reversal> needs a boundary qualification. The identity
$$
(h_t-\nu\nabla^2h)^2=h_t^2+\nu^2(\nabla^2h)^2+\nu\partial_t|\nabla h|^2-2\nu\nabla\cdot(h_t\nabla h)
$$
shows that the density is time-reversal invariant up to a <total divergence>. Thus the bulk stationary equation $h_{tt}-\nu^2\nabla^4h=0$ is invariant under $t\mapsto-t$, although the printed density is not pointwise unchanged. The <time reversal of the squared diffusion-residual action> exchanges the growing and decaying branches; the selected first-order <diffusion equation> alone is not time-reversal invariant at fixed positive $\nu$.

