= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The derivatives of the <Lagrangian density> are $\mathcal L_\phi=0$, $\mathcal L_{\phi_t}=\phi_t$, and $\mathcal L_{\phi_x}=-\phi_x$. The <Euler-Lagrange field equation> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\phi_{tt}-\phi_{xx}=0.}
$$
This is the <one-dimensional wave equation> with <wave speed> $c_s=\sqrt{\kappa/\rho}=1$. Both signs of propagation are allowed: $\phi(x,t)=F(x-t)+H(x+t)$ for sufficiently regular profiles.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use $x^\mu=(t,x)$ and <Minkowski metric> $\eta^{\mu\nu}=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$, with the wave speed equal to one. Regarding $\phi$ as a <scalar field>, the symmetric <canonical stress-energy tensor> is
$$
T^{\mu\nu}=\partial^\mu\phi\,\partial^\nu\phi-\eta^{\mu\nu}\mathcal L
=\begin{pmatrix}\mathcal E&p\\p&\mathcal E\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\mathcal E=\frac12(\phi_t^2+\phi_x^2),\quad p=-\phi_t\phi_x.
$$
It obeys $\partial_\mu T^{\mu\nu}=0$ on the <wave equation>. For a <scalar field> the intrinsic spin current vanishes. The total orbital <Lorentz current> and its integrated angular-momentum tensor are
$$
\boxed{\mathcal J^{\lambda\mu\nu}=x^\mu T^{\lambda\nu}-x^\nu T^{\lambda\mu},\qquad
J^{\mu\nu}(t)=\int_{\mathbb R}\mathcal J^{0\mu\nu}\,dx.}
$$
Because there is only one spatial dimension, its only independent possibly nonzero component is
$$
\boxed{J^{01}=\int_{\mathbb R}(t p-x\mathcal E)\,dx=tP-\int_{\mathbb R}x\mathcal E\,dx,\qquad J^{10}=-J^{01},}
$$
where $P=\int p\,dx$. The corresponding flux is $\mathcal J^{101}=t\mathcal E-xp$. Symmetry and conservation of $T$ give
$$
\partial_\lambda\mathcal J^{\lambda\mu\nu}=T^{\mu\nu}-T^{\nu\mu}=0.
$$
Thus $J^{01}$ is conserved provided the boundary flux vanishes and the integrals exist. For example, localized wave packets on an infinite line have this property. One can also check directly that $d\int x\mathcal E\,dx/dt=P$, so the two terms in $J^{01}$ have cancelling derivatives.

The significance is the <boost charge of a one-dimensional scalar field>. There is no independent spatial rotation or ordinary spatial angular-momentum component in one dimension. Instead, the action has the mathematical <Lorentz invariance> of a massless wave field with invariant speed $c_s=1$, and $J^{01}$ generates its boost. Its conservation says that the center of energy moves uniformly. This is a symmetry of the ideal wave-field model, rather than an assertion of fundamental vacuum Lorentz symmetry for a material rod. Fixed boundaries can break it, and a general solution with nonvanishing boundary flux need not conserve the integrated charge.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

With the PDF's inverse <Fourier transform> convention, the derivatives act as
$$
\partial_t\mapsto-i\omega,\qquad\partial_x\mapsto-ik,
$$
so its forced differential operator becomes $\omega^2-k^2+i\gamma\omega k^2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\widehat G(k,\omega)=\frac1{\omega^2-k^2+i\gamma k^2\omega}.}
$$
For the physical impulse response choose the <retarded fundamental solution>, vanishing before $t=0$. For $k\ne0$ and $\gamma>0$, its poles lie in the lower half-plane. The <retarded Fourier pole prescription> $\omega\mapsto\omega+i0$ specifies the zero mode and the zero-damping limit as well; a Green function without a temporal or boundary prescription would otherwise permit homogeneous additions.

This is the <Kelvin-Voigt rod Green function>. Multiplying the homogeneous real-space equation by $-1$ gives
$$
\phi_{tt}-\phi_{xx}-\gamma\phi_{txx}=0.
$$
A spatial <Fourier mode> therefore satisfies $\ddot\phi_k+\gamma k^2\dot\phi_k+k^2\phi_k=0$: positive $\gamma$ really damps the motion. For sufficiently decaying fields its <energy> satisfies
$$
\frac d{dt}\frac12\int(\phi_t^2+\phi_x^2)\,dx=-\gamma\int\phi_{tx}^2\,dx\leq0.
$$
This checks the sign of the imaginary term in $\widehat G$ independently of the transform calculation.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $0<k^2<4/\gamma^2$, define
$$
a_k=\frac{\gamma k^2}{2},\qquad \Omega_k=\sqrt{k^2-\frac{\gamma^2k^4}{4}}>0.
$$
The simple poles are $\omega_\pm=\pm\Omega_k-ia_k$. In the inverse transform $\widetilde G(k,t)=\int e^{-i\omega t}\widehat G\,d\omega/(2\pi)$, close the contour below for $t>0$. Its clockwise orientation gives $-i$ times the sum of residues. That sum is
$$
\frac{e^{-i\omega_+t}-e^{-i\omega_-t}}{2\Omega_k}
=-i e^{-a_kt}\frac{\sin(\Omega_kt)}{\Omega_k}.
$$
For $t<0$, close above; there are no poles. The <residue theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\widetilde G_R(k,t)=-\Theta(t)e^{-\gamma k^2t/2}\frac{\sin(\Omega_kt)}{\Omega_k}.}
$$
Here $\Theta$ is the <Heaviside step function>. At $k=0$ the continuous retarded limit is $-t\Theta(t)$. At critical damping, $\Omega_k\to0$ likewise replaces $\sin(\Omega_kt)/\Omega_k$ by $t$; beyond the stated oscillatory range an imaginary $\Omega_k$ converts it to a hyperbolic sine. These continuations retain decaying temporal exponents.

For each fixed $k$, taking $\gamma\to0^+$ gives
$$
\widetilde G_R(k,t)\longrightarrow-\Theta(t)\frac{\sin(|k|t)}{|k|}.
$$
For $t>0$, invert in space and set $s=kt$:
$$
G_R(x,t)=-\frac{\Theta(t)}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\sin(kt)}k e^{-ikx}\,dk
=-\frac{\Theta(t)}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\sin s}s e^{-isx/t}\,ds.
$$
The correctly normalized <sinc function> transform is
$$
\frac1\pi\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\sin s}s e^{-isu}\,ds
=\begin{cases}1,&|u|<1,\\0,&|u|>1.\end{cases}
$$
For instance, $\sin s/s=\frac12\int_{-1}^{1}e^{isy}\,dy$, and <Fourier inversion> immediately gives the factor $\pi$. The PDF's prefactor $\sqrt{2/\pi}$ is inconsistent with its top hat of unit height; it would give height $\sqrt{2\pi}$. Using the normalized identity yields
$$
\boxed{G_R(x,t)=-\frac12\Theta(t)\Theta(t-|x|)
=-\frac12\Theta(t-|x|),}
$$
as a <distribution>. The values assigned precisely at a wave front do not change that distribution. This is the negative of the standard <one-dimensional retarded wave Green function>, because the PDF's differential operator is $-\partial_t^2+\partial_x^2$.

The sign and initial data can be checked without any contour calculation. Integrating the spatial-Fourier equation through the impulse gives
$$
\widetilde G_R(k,0^+)=0,\qquad \partial_t\widetilde G_R(k,0^+)=-1.
$$
In real space these are $G_R(x,0^+)=0$ and $\partial_tG_R(x,0^+)=-\delta(x)$. In particular,
$$
\lim_{t\downarrow0}\int_{\mathbb R}\frac{G_R(x,t)}t f(x)\,dx=-f(0)
$$
for a smooth test function $f$, confirming the negative delta-function initial velocity. Before the impulse the displacement and velocity are zero. The disturbance fills the forward <causal cone for the constant-speed wave equation>, whose fronts move at unit speed, consistent with $\rho=\kappa=1$. The final question's printed $\rho=k$ is interpreted as $\rho=\kappa$, referring to the elastic constant, not to the Fourier wavevector. No initial conditions were otherwise specified, so the causal choice has been made explicitly.

\Image[2018-q4-retarded-rod-response.png]
{title=Retarded rod response: damping of a Fourier mode and the undamped causal cone}
{description=Original calculated Fourier-mode responses for $k=1$, and the zero-damping spacetime Green function for the operator $-\partial_t^2+\partial_x^2$. Its value is $-1/2$ inside the forward cone and zero outside; the negative sign enforces the stated positive delta-function source.}
